    "focal_turn_index": 3,
    "prior_context": {
      "alternative_citation_allowed": true,
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 2 USER]\ne020\tI found her phone on the couch, it was unlocked.\ne021\tI opened Instagram.\ne022\tThe account was under a fake name.\ne023\tI saw the messages with this guy and I just — I screamed at her.\ne024\tI led with the guy.\ne025\t\"Who is this person?\ne026\tHow old is he?\ne027\tWhat have you done?\"\ne028\tShe just cried and ran upstairs.\ne029\tThat was three days ago.\ne030\tShe's only come out to use the bathroom and grab food when I'm not looking.\ne031\tShe won't look at me.\n[Turn 2 ASSISTANT]\ne032\tOK.\ne033\tThe way you confronted her matters because it shapes what happens next.\ne034\tYou led with the DMs and your fear, which is completely understandable, but from her perspective the message she received was \"my mom invaded my privacy and is terrified of me.\"\ne035\tTeenagers in shame-spirals lock down harder when they feel judged.\ne036\tShe's not hiding from you because she's guilty of something worse; she's hiding because she feels like she's been seen at her most humiliating moment and she doesn't know how to come out of that.\ne037\tThe first move here isn't to push on the 19-year-old.\ne038\tIt's to create a way for her to re-enter the relationship without losing all face.\ne039\tSomething like: slide a note under her door that says \"I love you.\ne040\tI am not going anywhere.\ne041\tWhen you're ready, I want to hear your side.\ne042\tWe can figure this out together.\"\ne043\tDon't include rules, conditions, or threats.\ne044\tJust that.\ne045\tThe reason I'm saying this before we talk about the guy: if she doesn't start talking to you, nothing else you do will land.\ne046\tThe 19-year-old is the bigger problem medium-term, but in the next 48 hours, the priority is re-opening communication.",
